42":{"l":"λιμός","x":"limós","d":"a scarcity of food","p":null,"t":"limos","g":"a famine","fb":[],"v":[23963,24724,25087,25601,25604,25836,27126,27334,28149,29011,30796,30996]},"G3063":{"l":"λοιπόν","x":"loipón","d":"something remaining (adverbially)","p":null,"t":"loipon","g":"Finally","fb":[],"v":[24098,24794,27873,28374,28430,28511,29049,29416,29444,29598,29673,29872,30140]},"G3003":{"l":"λεγεών","x":"legeṓn","d":"a \"legion\", i.e. Roman regiment (figuratively)","p":null,"t":"legeon","g":"Legion","fb":[],"v":[24106,24372,24378,25274]},"G3094":{"l":"Μαγδαληνή","x":"Magdalēnḗ","d":"a female Magdalene, i.e. inhabitant of Magdala","p":null,"t":"magdalene","g":"Magdalene","fb":[["magdalene","Magdalene","Magadan","It means \"from Magdala,\" the town on the Sea of Galilee, and it keeps this Mary apart from the other Marys in the Gospels. She watched the crucifixion and was the first to see the risen Jesus. The old idea that she was the sinful woman of Luke 7 has nothing in the text behind it.","place",12]],"v":[24184,24189,24195,24865,24872,24873,24881,25246,26000,26849,26867,26884]},"G3017":{"l":"Λευΐ","x":"Leuḯ","d":"Levi, the name of three Israelites","p":null,"t":"leui","g":"of Levi","fb":[["levi","Levi","Jacob's son living at the time of the Patriarchs","Jacob's third son. He and Simeon massacred the men of Shechem after their sister Dinah was raped, and Jacob cursed them both for it on his deathbed. Even so, Levi's descendants became Israel's priestly tribe, given service at the tabernacle instead of land, partly for their zeal against the golden calf at Sinai.","person",43],["matthew-2","Matthew","Apostle living at the time of the New Testament","Mark and Luke name him twice, and he is a man from the New Testament.","person",2],["levi-3","Levi","Man living at the time of Divided Monarchy","This is Matthew the tax collector. Mark and Luke call him Levi when they tell the story of his calling.","person",1],["levi-2","Levi","Man living at the time of Exile and Return","A Levi in Luke's genealogy, and not Levi the tax collector.","person",1]],"v":[24273,25048,25053,25133,30063,30067,30812]},"G3021":{"l":"λευκαίνω","x":"leukaínō","d":"to whiten","p":null,"t":"leukaino","g":"to whiten","fb":[],"v":[24540,30819]},"G3016":{"l":"λεπτόν","x":"leptón","d":"something scaled (light), i.e. a small coin","p":null,"t":"lepton","g":"lepta","fb":[],"v":[24714,25517,25827]},"G3009":{"l":"λειτουργία","x":"leitourgía","d":"public function (as priest (\"liturgy\") or almsgiver)","p":null,"t":"leitourgia","g":"service","fb":[],"v":[24915,28963,29402,29415,30092,30120]},"G3085":{"l":"λύτρωσις","x":"lýtrōsis","d":"a ransoming (figuratively)","p":null,"t":"lytrosis","g":"redemption","fb":[],"v":[24960,25010,30111]},"G3078":{"l":"Λυσανίας","x":"Lysanías","d":"grief-dispelling; Lysanias, a governor of Abilene","p":null,"t":"lysanias","g":"Lysanias","fb":[["lysanias","Lysanias","Tetrarch of Abilene living at the time of the New Testament","Tetrarch of Abilene, on the eastern slope of the Anti-Lebanon near Damascus, and one of the rulers Luke uses to date his story. Josephus confirms the tetrarchy was real.","person",1]],"v":[25025]},"G3006":{"l":"λεῖος","x":"leîos","d":"smooth, i.e. \"level\"","p":null,"t":"leios","g":"smooth","fb":[],"v":[25029]},"G3092":{"l":"Μαάθ","x":"Maáth","d":"Maath, an Israelite","p":null,"t":"maath","g":"of Maath","fb":[["maath","Maath","Man living at the time of Exile and Return","One name in Luke's genealogy of Jesus, between the exile and Joseph.","person",1]],"v":[25050]},"G3041":{"l":"λίμνη","x":"límnē","d":"a pond (large or small)","p":null,"t":"limne","g":"lake","fb":[],"v":[25107,25108,25266,25267,25277,31032,31043,31047,31047,31048,31056]},"G3018":{"l":"Λευΐς","x":"Leuḯs","d":"Lewis (i.e. Levi), a Christian","p":null,"t":"leuis","g":"Levi","fb":[["levi-4","Levi","Apostle living at the time of the New Testament","The tax collector better known as Matthew. One verse after Jesus called him from his booth, Levi threw a big dinner for him, full of tax collectors and other outcasts, and the Pharisees complained.","person",1]],"v":[25135]},"G3019":{"l":"Λευΐτης","x":"Leuḯtēs","d":"a Levite, i.e. descendant of Levi","p":null,"t":"leuites","g":"a Levite","fb":[["levite","Levite","Jacob's son living at the time of the Patriarchs","They belonged to the tribe of Levi and served at the tabernacle and later the temple, helping the priests, who came from Aaron's branch of the same tribe. Their work was religious, so they got no tribal land, only forty eight towns among the other tribes and the tithes of Israel. Over time they carried and guarded the holy things, sang, kept the gates, and taught the law.","person",7]],"v":[25394,26062,27057]},"G3081":{"l":"λυσιτελεῖ","x":"lysiteleî","d":"impersonally, it answers the purpose, i.e. is advantageous","p":null,"t":"lysitelei","g":"It is better","fb":[],"v":[25652]},"G3091":{"l":"Λώτ","x":"Lṓt","d":"Lot, a patriarch","p":null,"t":"lot","g":"Lot","fb":[["lot","Lot","Man living at the time of the Patriarchs","Abraham's nephew, who came with him from Ur. When their herdsmen started fighting and they split, Lot took the green Jordan valley and ended up living by Sodom. Angels dragged him out before it burned, and his wife looked back and became a pillar of salt. Afterwards his two daughters got him drunk and had sons by him, the fathers of Moab and Ammon.","person",34]],"v":[25678,25679,25682,30501]},"G3007":{"l":"λείπω","x":"leípō","d":"to leave, i.e. (intransitively or passively) to fail or be absent","p":null,"t":"leipo","g":"lacking","fb":[],"v":[25709,29891,29930,30264,30265,30302]},"G3061":{"l":"λοιμός","x":"loimós","d":"a plague (literally, the disease, or figuratively, a pest)","p":null,"t":"loimos","g":"a pest","fb":[],"v":[25836,27772]},"G3049":{"l":"λογίζομαι","x":"logízomai","d":"to take an inventory, i.e. estimate (literally or figuratively)","p":null,"t":"logizomai","g":"reckoning","fb":[],"v":[25900,26572,27611,27963,27986,28017,28023,28024,28025,28026,28028,28029,28030,28031,28042,28043,28044,28077,28132,28150,28161,28292,28429,28665,28671,28841,28891,28968,28968,28973,28977,28989,29023,29102,29428,29444,29880,30185,30310,30471]},"G3077":{"l":"λύπη","x":"lýpē","d":"sadness","p":null,"t":"lype","g":"grief","fb":[],"v":[25908,26731,26745,26746,26747,28155,28820,28822,28826,28921,28921,28958,29412,29412,30217,30412]},"G3026":{"l":"λῆρος","x":"lēros","d":"twaddle, i.e. an incredible story","p":null,"t":"leros","g":"folly","fb":[],"v":[26001]},"G3084":{"l":"λυτρόω","x":"lytróō","d":"to ransom (literally or figuratively)","p":null,"t":"lytroo","g":"you were redeemed","fb":[],"v":[26011,29916,30386]},"G3035":{"l":"λίθινος","x":"líthinos","d":"stony, i.e. made of stone","p":null,"t":"lithinos","g":"of stone","fb":[],"v":[26100,28839,30855]},"G3058":{"l":"λοιδορέω","x":"loidoréō","d":"to reproach, i.e. vilify","p":null,"t":"loidoreo","g":"Being reviled","fb":[],"v":[26467,27736,28440,30416]},"G3034":{"l":"λιθάζω","x":"litházō","d":"to lapidate","p":null,"t":"lithazo","g":"I was stoned","fb":[],"v":[26511,26512,26513,26530,27084,27432,29009,30203]},"G3046":{"l":"λίτρα","x":"lítra","d":"a pound in weight","p":null,"t":"litra","g":"a litra","fb":[],"v":[26582,26863]},"G3012":{"l":"λέντιον","x":"léntion","d":"a \"linen\" cloth, i.e. apron","p":null,"t":"lention","g":"a towel","fb":[],"v":[26633,26634]},"G3068":{"l":"λούω","x":"loúō","d":"to bathe (the whole person; whereas G3538 (νίπτω) means to wet a part only, and G4150 (πλύνω) to wash, cleanse garments exclusively)","p":null,"t":"louo","g":"having washed","fb":[],"v":[26639,27252,27515,30149,30516]},"G3038":{"l":"λιθόστρωτος","x":"lithóstrōtos","d":"stone-strewed, i.e. a tessellated mosaic on which the Roman tribunal was placed","p":null,"t":"lithostrotos","g":"[the] Stone Pavement","fb":[["pavement","Pavement","Gabbatha","A raised stone platform outside Pilate's headquarters in Jerusalem, called Gabbatha in Aramaic. Roman governors set the judgment seat there to hear cases in public. Pilate sat there at the trial of Jesus (John 19:13).","place",1]],"v":[26837]},"G3057":{"l":"λόγχη","x":"lónchē","d":"a \"lance\"","p":null,"t":"lonche","g":"with a spear","fb":[],"v":[26858]},"G3033":{"l":"Λιβύη","x":"Libýē","d":"Libye, a region of Africa","p":null,"t":"libye","g":"of Libya","fb":[["libya","Libya","","North Africa west of Egypt, with Cyrene its main city in the Greco-Roman period. People from Libya were in Jerusalem at Pentecost.","place",1]],"v":[26958]},"G3032":{"l":"Λιβερτῖνος","x":"Libertînos","d":"a Roman freedman","p":null,"t":"libertinos","g":"Freedmen","fb":[["libertine","Libertine","Group of Jews in the New Testament","Members of the Synagogue of the Freedmen in Jerusalem, Jews once enslaved in Rome and later set free, possibly taken there after Pompey conquered Judea in 63 BC. Some of them argued with Stephen and helped bring the false charges that got him killed.","group",1]],"v":[27109]},"G3099":{"l":"Μαδιάν","x":"Madián","d":"Madian (i.e. Midian), a region of Arabia","p":null,"t":"madian","g":"of Midian","fb":[["madian","Madian","Midian","Midian, in Greek. Moses ran there after killing an Egyptian and kept sheep for his father-in-law until God called him at the burning bush.","place",1]],"v":[27144]},"G3086":{"l":"λυτρωτής","x":"lytrōtḗs","d":"a redeemer (figuratively)","p":null,"t":"lytrotes","g":"redeemer","fb":[],"v":[27150]},"G3051":{"l":"λόγιον","x":"lógion","d":"an utterance (of God)","p":null,"t":"logion","g":"oracles","fb":[],"v":[27153,27991,30036,30451]},"G3075":{"l":"λυμαίνομαι","x":"lymaínomai","d":"properly, to soil, i.e. (figuratively) insult (maltreat)","p":null,"t":"lymainomai","g":"was destroying","fb":[],"v":[27178]},"G3096":{"l":"μαγεύω","x":"mageúō","d":"to practice magic","p":null,"t":"mageuo","g":"practicing sorcery","fb":[],"v":[27184]},"G3095":{"l":"μαγεία","x":"mageía","d":"\"magic\"","p":null,"t":"mageia","g":"magic arts","fb":[],"v":[27186]},"G3013":{"l":"λεπίς","x":"lepís","d":"a flake","p":null,"t":"lepis","g":"scales","fb":[],"v":[27233]},"G3069":{"l":"Λύδδα","x":"Lýdda","d":"Lydda (i.e. Lod), a place in Palestine","p":null,"t":"lydda","g":"Lydda","fb":[["lydda","Lydda","Lod","About nine miles southeast of Joppa on the coastal plain. Peter healed a paralysed man named Aeneas there, and many in the plain of Sharon turned to the Lord. Today it is Lod, where Ben Gurion Airport is.","place",3]],"v":[27247,27250,27253]},"G3066":{"l":"Λούκιος","x":"Loúkios","d":"illuminative; Lucius, a Christian","p":null,"t":"loukios","g":"Lucius","fb":[["lucius","Lucius","Man living at the time of the New Testament","One of the prophets and teachers in the church at Antioch, from Cyrene, there when the Holy Spirit set Barnabas and Saul apart. Paul sends greetings from a Lucius he calls his kinsman in Romans 16:21, and whether that is the same man is uncertain.","person",2]],"v":[27362,28355]},"G3008":{"l":"λειτουργέω","x":"leitourgéō","d":"to be a public servant, i.e. (by analogy) to perform religious or charitable functions (worship, obey, relieve)","p":null,"t":"leitourgeo","g":"As were ministering","fb":[],"v":[27363,28328,30138]},"G3071":{"l":"Λυκαονία","x":"Lykaonía","d":"Lycaonia, a region of Asia Minor","p":null,"t":"lykaonia","g":"of Lycaonia","fb":[["lycaonia","Lycaonia","","Inland Asia Minor, between Cappadocia and Galatia, with Iconium, Lystra and Derbe in it. Paul and Barnabas preached through it on the first journey. The crowd at Lystra shouted in \"the speech of Lycaonia\" when they took the two for Hermes and Zeus, and an inscription found near Lystra shows those two gods really were worshipped together there.","place",1]],"v":[27419]},"G3082":{"l":"Λύστρα","x":"Lýstra","d":"Lystra, a place in Asia Minor","p":null,"t":"lystra","g":"Lystra","fb":[["lystra","Lystra","","A Roman colony in the wild hills of Lycaonia, and Timothy's hometown. Paul healed a man lame from birth there and the crowd tried to sacrifice to him and Barnabas as Hermes and Zeus. Then Paul's opponents turned the same crowd, and they stoned him and left him for dead.","place",6]],"v":[27419,27421,27434,27483,27484,29858]},"G3072":{"l":"Λυκαονιστί","x":"Lykaonistí","d":"Lycaonistically, i.e. in the language of the Lycaonians","p":null,"t":"lykaonisti","g":"in Lycaonian","fb":[],"v":[27424]},"G3070":{"l":"Λυδία","x":"Lydía","d":"Lydia, a Christian woman","p":null,"t":"lydia","g":"Lydia","fb":[["lydia","Lydia","Woman living at the time of the New Testament","She sold purple cloth, came from Thyatira, and lived in Philippi when Paul arrived. She already worshipped God, and after hearing Paul by the river she and her household were baptised, apparently the first converts in Europe. Then she put Paul and his companions up in her house.","person",2]],"v":[27496,27522]},"G3052":{"l":"λόγιος","x":"lógios","d":"fluent, i.e. an orator","p":null,"t":"logios","g":"eloquent","fb":[],"v":[27580]},"G3079":{"l":"Λυσίας","x":"Lysías","d":"Lysias, a Roman","p":null,"t":"lysias","g":"Lysias","fb":[["lysias","Lysias","Man living at the time of the New Testament","Claudius Lysias, the Roman tribune over the garrison at the Antonia Fortress. He pulled Paul out of a mob in the temple and then sent him under armed guard to Felix in Caesarea with a letter about the case.","person",2]],"v":[27758,27789]},"G3073":{"l":"Λυκία","x":"Lykía","d":"Lycia, a province of Asia Minor","p":null,"t":"lykia","g":"of Lycia","fb":[["lycia","Lycia","","Mountains and coast in southwest Asia Minor, across from Rhodes. Paul's ships put in at its ports, Patara and Myra.","place",1]],"v":[27858]},"G3040":{"l":"λιμήν","x":"limḗn","d":"a harbor","p":null,"t":"limen","g":"Havens","fb":[],"v":[27861,27865,27865]},"G3047":{"l":"λίψ","x":"líps","d":"the south(- west) wind (as bringing rain, i.e. (by extension) the south quarter)","p":null,"t":"lips","g":"[the] southwest","fb":[],"v":[27865]},"G3053":{"l":"λογισμός","x":"logismós","d":"computation, i.e. (figuratively) reasoning (conscience, conceit)","p":null,"t":"logismos","g":"arguments","fb":[],"v":[27975,28970]},"G3005":{"l":"λεῖμμα","x":"leîmma","d":"a remainder","p":null,"t":"leimma","g":"a remnant","fb":[],"v":[28212]},"G3050":{"l":"λογικός","x":"logikós","d":"rational (\"logical\")","p":null,"t":"logikos","g":"reasonable","fb":[],"v":[28244,30395]},"G3011":{"l":"λειτουργός","x":"leitourgós","d":"a public servant, i.e. a functionary in the Temple or Gospel, or (genitive case) a worshipper (of God) or benefactor (of man)","p":null,"t":"leitourgos","g":"a minister","fb":[],"v":[28270,28317,29410,29964,30088]},"G3060":{"l":"λοίδορος","x":"loídoros","d":"abusive, i.e. a blackguard","p":null,"t":"loidoros","g":"verbal abuser","fb":[],"v":[28460,28472]},"G3080":{"l":"λύσις","x":"lýsis","d":"a loosening, i.e. (specially), divorce","p":null,"t":"lysis","g":"to be loosed","fb":[],"v":[28509]},"G3048":{"l":"λογία","x":"logía","d":"a contribution","p":null,"t":"logia","g":"collection","fb":[],"v":[28772,28773]},"G3064":{"l":"λοιποῦ","x":"loipoû","d":"remaining time","p":null,"t":"loipou","g":"Henceforth","fb":[],"v":[29199,29341]},"G3067":{"l":"λουτρόν","x":"loutrón","d":"a bath, i.e. (figuratively), baptism","p":null,"t":"loutron","g":"washing","fb":[],"v":[29324,29922]},"G3028":{"l":"λῆμψις","x":"lēmpsis","d":"receipt (the act)","p":null,"t":"lempsis","g":"receiving","fb":[],"v":[29451]},"G3065":{"l":"Λουκᾶς","x":"Loukâs","d":"Lucas, a Christian","p":null,"t":"loukas","g":"Luke","fb":[["lucas","Lucas","Man living at the time of the New Testament","The Latin form of Luke, Paul's travelling companion and doctor. Early tradition gives him the Gospel of Luke and Acts, which would make him the one Gentile writer in the New Testament.","person",3]],"v":[29550,29875,29956]},"G3059":{"l":"λοιδορία","x":"loidoría","d":"slander or vituperation","p":null,"t":"loidoria","g":"insult","fb":[],"v":[29771,30427,30427]},"G3055":{"l":"λογομαχία","x":"logomachía","d":"disputation about trifles (\"logomachy\")","p":null,"t":"logomachia","g":"disputes about words","fb":[],"v":[29786]},"G3090":{"l":"Λωΐς","x":"Lōḯs","d":"Lois, a Christian woman","p":null,"t":"lois","g":"Lois","fb":[["lois","Lois","Woman living at the time of the New Testament","Timothy's grandmother. Paul says the faith he sees in Timothy lived first in her and then in her daughter Eunice.","person",1]],"v":[29808]},"G3054":{"l":"λογομαχέω","x":"logomachéō","d":"to be disputatious (on trifles)","p":null,"t":"logomacheo","g":"to quarrel about words","fb":[],"v":[29835]},"G3023":{"l":"λέων","x":"léōn","d":"a \"lion\"","p":null,"t":"leon","g":"a lion","fb":[],"v":[29881,30199,30467,30770,30779,30843,30852,30859,30905]},"G3044":{"l":"Λίνος","x":"Línos","d":"Linus, a Christian","p":null,"t":"linos","g":"Linus","fb":[["linus","Linus","Man living at the time of the New Testament","Paul passes on his greeting to Timothy from Rome. Irenaeus records a tradition that he was the first bishop of Rome after Peter and Paul, though the New Testament says nothing about any office he held.","person",1]],"v":[29885]},"G3010":{"l":"λειτουργικός","x":"leitourgikós","d":"functional publicly (\"liturgic\"); i.e. beneficient","p":null,"t":"leitourgikos","g":"ministering","fb":[],"v":[29971]},"G3020":{"l":"Λευϊτικός","x":"Leuïtikós","d":"Levitic, i.e. relating to the Levites","p":null,"t":"leuitikos","g":"Levitical","fb":[["levitical","Levitical","Jacob's son living at the time of the Patriarchs","Not a person. The word describes the hereditary priesthood from Levi, and Hebrews 7:11 uses it to argue that this priesthood could never make anything perfect, since Scripture speaks of another priest \"after the order of Melchizedek.\"","term",1]],"v":[30069]},"G3024":{"l":"λήθη","x":"lḗthē","d":"forgetfulness","p":null,"t":"lethe","g":"forgetfulness","fb":[],"v":[30482]},"G3031":{"l":"λιβανωτός","x":"libanōtós","d":"frankincense, i.e. (by extension) a censer for burning it","p":null,"t":"libanotos","g":"a censer","fb":[],"v":[30825,30827]},"G3045":{"l":"λιπαρός","x":"liparós","d":"fat, i.e. (figuratively) sumptuous","p":null,"t":"liparos","g":"sumptuous things","fb":[],"v":[31002]},"G3098":{"l":"Μαγώγ","x":"Magṓg","d":"Magog, a foreign nation, i.e. (figuratively) an Antichristian party","p":null,"t":"magog","g":"Magog","fb":[["magog","Magog","","Ezekiel makes it the homeland of Gog, who leads a great army against a restored Israel. Revelation picks the name up again for the nations gathered in the last rebellion against God. In the genealogies Magog is a son of Japheth.","place",3],["magog-2","Magog","Man living at the time of the Patriarchs","Genesis and 1 Chronicles name him twice, and he is a man from the patriarchs.","person",2]],"v":[31041]}}